INT. NIGHT BUS - MOVING - NIGHT

Rain on glass. Neon smears past.

A battered double-decker, half empty, rattling through a city that does not appear on any map. Flickering strip lights. Vinyl seats slashed and taped. A bell that rings by itself.

Two teenagers share earbuds in the back row. A scruffy terrier sleeps under a seat.

Up front, NELL (20s, soot on her cheek, a sword wrapped in a rain poncho) grips the pole.

HOODIE GUY sits across the aisle. Grey hood. No face. Just a slow, steady grin in the dark.

The engine COUGHS.

NELL
(to the whole bus)
Nobody panic. We're ahead of them.

BARROW
(a hulking man in a bus-driver's cap, hauling the wheel)
Define ahead.

BELL. DING. DING.

NELL
(muttered)
Come on, come on.

The lights DIE. Back on. Silence.

Rain. Nothing.

IRIS
(clutching a lantern, eyes on the roof)
Something just landed up there.

THUMP. THUMP. THUMP.

The teenagers pull out their earbuds. The dog lifts its head.

BARROW
(hands off the wheel, cracking his knuckles)
Hold this thing steady.

NELL
(drawing the sword, glaring at the ceiling)
You're not steering?

BARROW
(grinning)
Not anymore.

CRUNCH. A claw punches through the roof.

HOODIE GUY
(rising, pushing back his hood, calm)
Let me.

A flash of silver light. A SHRIEK from above. Then a heavy body slides off the roof and is gone.

Silence. Rain.

IRIS
(whispering)
Who are you?

HOODIE GUY
(sitting back down)
Cass. Once upon a time, I was the one they sent you to find.

NELL
(lowering the sword, to herself)
Of course you were.

The bus hums on into the dark. The dog goes back to sleep.
